# Board defaults for nvidia-generic.
#
# The parent is amd64-generic:kernel-6_12, not amd64-generic:base. That profile's own
# parent chain is ../base plus chromiumos:features/kernel/deselect-all-kernels, so it
# takes amd64-generic's settings, turns every kernel USE flag off, then enables
# kernel-6_12. Inheriting :base instead would give kernel 5.15, which is what
# amd64-generic/profiles/base/make.defaults selects.
#
# 6.12 is chosen because it is what patches/kernel/ was written against, and because the
# NVIDIA 595.91.07 open modules are a 2025 release. NVIDIA's own floor is much lower
# ("Linux kernel 4.15 or newer" per their README), so this is about matching our own
# config work and having modern DRM, not about a hard requirement.

# Mesa drivers to build. The iGPU path (amdgpu/radeonsi) is kept because it is the
# recovery console and must always work -- Stage 2 deliberately keeps the display on
# the iGPU while the NVIDIA driver is brought up.
#
# amd64-generic ships VIDEO_CARDS="intel llvmpipe nouveau radeon" with no amdgpu at all,
# and radeon is the pre-GCN driver which will not bind to this machine's Raphael iGPU
# [1002:164e]. Overriding this is what makes an iGPU console possible.
#
# nouveau is included as a *diagnostic* fallback, not the intended driver. If the
# NVIDIA module misbehaves it lets us prove PCI enumeration, DRM/KMS and modesetting
# independently. minigbm already ships a nouveau dumb-buffer backend, so this path
# works without patches, which makes it a useful control experiment.
#
# llvmpipe is kept deliberately. This is not a stylistic carry-over from amd64-generic: in
# media-libs/mesa it maps to "gallium_enable video_cards_llvmpipe swrast", so it is the
# software rasteriser. It is the only GL that needs no working kernel driver at all, which
# makes it the last line of the recovery story -- exactly the case where amdgpu has failed
# and dropping it would leave nothing. VIDEO_CARDS is a plain assignment in
# amd64-generic/profiles/base/make.defaults, so omitting llvmpipe here would remove it.
#
# radeon stays in VIDEO_CARDS even though the kernel radeon driver cannot bind to Raphael.
# mesa-amd's meson still requires pkg-config libdrm_radeon (media-libs/mesa-amd-23.0.2
# meson.build:1755), and libdrm only builds that .pc when video_cards_radeon is on.
# chipset-cezanne has the same pair: VIDEO_CARDS="-* radeon amdgpu". intel is dropped
# because there is no Intel graphics on this machine. The leading -* is the idiom the
# in-tree AMD boards use and makes the clearing of inherited values explicit.
#
# Note what VIDEO_CARDS does NOT do here: it does not choose the GL implementation. See
# virtual/opengles and virtual/vulkan-icd in this overlay -- ChromeOS ships split mesa
# packages and the choice is made there. VIDEO_CARDS still matters for minigbm's backends,
# for LICENSE selection, and for packages that gate on video_cards_* directly.
VIDEO_CARDS="-* amdgpu radeonsi radeon nouveau llvmpipe"

# LLVM_TARGETS is deliberately not set. amd64-generic already has "AMDGPU X86", which is
# what both mesa-amd (radeonsi needs the AMDGPU backend) and mesa-llvmpipe (needs X86) want.
# chipset-cezanne narrows it to "AMDGPU" alone, which is why that board cannot fall back to
# llvmpipe; inheriting the wider set is strictly better for us.

# Board-specific kernel config. cros-kernel.eclass passes this name to
# chromeos/scripts/prepareconfig, which builds .config by concatenating
#   chromeos/config/chromeos/base.config
#   chromeos/config/chromeos/x86_64/common.config
#   chromeos/config/chromeos/x86_64/<this name>.flavour.config
# in that order, so the flavour config wins on any duplicate symbol.
#
# The file is kept in this repo under overlay/overlay-nvidia-generic/kernel-config/ and
# installed into the kernel tree by sdk/build.sh, because the kernel tree is a synced
# repo rather than something we own.
CHROMEOS_KERNEL_SPLITCONFIG="chromiumos-x86_64-nvidia"

# Pull the NVIDIA stack into the image via the board BSP package.
USE="${USE} nvidia_open"

# glvnd is how a system carries more than one GL/EGL implementation at once: the vendor
# neutral libEGL.so dispatches to an implementation chosen by vendor JSON files under
# __EGL_VENDOR_LIBRARY_DIRS. Chrome needs Mesa for the iGPU and NVIDIA's own libEGL for
# the RTX, so without glvnd they would collide over the same soname. reven sets this for
# the same reason; amd64-generic does not.
USE="${USE} libglvnd"

# Flags every in-tree AMD board sets that amd64-generic does not, because amd64-generic is
# a VM target rather than real AMD hardware.
#
# amd_cpu identifies the CPU vendor to packages that branch on it; this is a Ryzen 9 7900X.
USE="${USE} amd_cpu"

# GpuMemoryBuffers backed by dma-bufs. Without it Chrome falls back to shared-memory
# transfers, which is a copy per frame and defeats the point of a discrete GPU.
USE="${USE} native_gpu_memory_buffers"

# amd64-generic explicitly sets -gpu_sandbox_start_early. Every real AMD board turns it back
# on, because the GPU process spawns threads early and the sandbox has to be in place first.
USE="${USE} gpu_sandbox_start_early"

# cros-kernel.eclass treats these as optional kernel fragments. If they are off, it
# *disables* CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE / CONFIG_VT after prepareconfig, which is what
# happened on the first nvidia-generic kernel build ("disabling framebuffer console",
# "disabling VT console"). Without both of these there is no text console on any GPU,
# which is the entire dGPU-first milestone. Flavour config setting them y is not enough.
USE="${USE} fbconsole vtconsole"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cryptohome / TPM (dual-boot with Ubuntu owning the hardware TPM)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# amd64-generic already selects a software TPM2 simulator + vtpm_proxy. Affirm those
# flags here (openFyde PC boards do the same) so a parent-profile change cannot silently
# re-enable the hardware TPM path and break vault mounts on this machine.
#
# Do NOT enable tpm2_simulator_deprecated / tpm_insecure_fallback unless diagnosis shows
# the modern simulator stack is insufficient — those are the older openFyde lockbox path.
USE="${USE} -tpm_dynamic -tpm tpm2 tpm2_simulator tpm2_simulator_manufacturer"
USE="${USE} vtpm_proxy"

# Land on the login pod instead of the full OOBE wizard. Combined with
# chrome_dev.conf.auth (nvidia-generic-auth) this avoids a Gaia-only brick when API keys
# are absent. Local vaults are still created via create-local-account.sh (SSH) until a
# chromeos-chrome OOBE button exists.
USE="${USE} oobe_skip_to_login"

# Crostini / Linux development environment host side. Inherited from amd64-generic
# (containers kvm_host crosvm-gpu virtio_gpu); affirmed so a parent-profile change cannot
# silently drop VM support. Guest sessions still gray out Linux — that is ChromeOS policy,
# not a missing USE flag. Persistent local users get Settings → Linux after login.
USE="${USE} containers kvm_host"

# AMD CPU microcode, for the Ryzen 9 7900X. amd64-generic sets LINUX_FIRMWARE="iwlwifi-all"
# as a plain assignment, so this appends rather than replacing it.
#
# There is deliberately no "amdgpu" here. sys-kernel/linux-firmware's IUSE_LINUX_FIRMWARE
# offers only amd_ucode, amdgpu_gc_11_0_1, amdgpu_gc_11_0_4 and amdgpu_yellow_carp -- there
# is no general amdgpu flag, and video_cards_amdgpu only selects a LICENSE entry. So no USE
# flag can install GPU firmware for this iGPU, and adding "amdgpu" would be a silently
# ignored value that reads as though it did something.
#
# The iGPU's firmware therefore comes from sys-firmware/amdgpu-raphael-firmware in this
# overlay, pulled in unconditionally by the board BSP.
LINUX_FIRMWARE="${LINUX_FIRMWARE} amd_ucode"
